and has been found in numerous widely separated localities. Vanadium resembles titanium in that it has been detected in various clays and igneous rocks. It is obtained in some quantity from the cupriferous Triassic beds of the vicinity of Mottram, Cheshire, England, in the form of the so-called mottramite, a hydrous vanadate of copper and lead.—'''Vanadium bronze,''' a fine yellow pigment employed in the place of gold bronze. It is an acid derivative of vanadium.}}

