Sp. ''Vándalo'' = Pg. ''Vandalo'' = G. ''Vandale'' = D. ''Wandel'' = Sw. Dan. ''Vandal'', < LL. ''Vandali'', also ''Vinduli'', ''Vindili'', Vandals, ''Vandalus'', adj., Vandal; from the Teut. name seen in D. ''Wenden'' = Icel. ''Vindir'', the Wends: see ''Wend''<sup>2</sup>.] '''I.''' ''n.'' '''1.''' One of a Germanic race who first appeared in middle and southern Germany, and in the first half of the fifth century ravaged Gaul, Spain, northern Africa, etc., and in 455 Rome itself, with enormous damage to accumulated treasures of art and literature. Hence—'''2.''' [''l. c.''] One who wilfully or ignorantly destroys or disfigures any work of art, literature, or the like; one who is hostile to or wantonly attacks anything that is beautiful or venerable.

'''II.''' ''a.'' [''l. c.''] Of or pertaining to a vandal or vandalism.
